      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Société française de pharmacologie et de thérapeutique. Published by Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "In Europe, breast cancer is the most common cancer among women. His treatment (TTT) can have a significant impact on daily life."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIMS",
            "content": "Explore the experienced short-term side effects (SE) of chemotherapy and hormonal therapy by women themselves, the information delivered and the place of the general practitioner (GP)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHOD",
            "content": "Qualitative study by individual interview, conducted from January to May 2015, with 14 women followed for no metastatic breast cancer after chemotherapy and possible initiation of hormone therapy, at the Cancer Institute of the Loire (France)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The SE of chemotherapy, except vomiting, have appeared very trying (fatigue, pains oral and cutaneous involvement), punctuated and accentuated by the cures. They were resounding on daily life, requiring family or professional assistance. Falling dander, considered stigmatizing, violated the person and femininity. The women have estimated to be well informed. They had appreciated the initial response of the pivot nurse. The least expected SE of hormone therapy, hot flashes and articular pains might limit activities. To counteract these side effects, mostly accepted to prevent recidivism, a remedy for complementary therapies was frequent. The GP was solicited for some SE, sometimes for complementary medicine (CM)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Over the cures, women suggested support meetings, a more sustainable action of the pivot nurse, a better attention to their complaints and a more personalized information. The development of TTT less EI provider was desired. The effect of CM on SE should be explored."
          }
        ]
      },
